# cine act=rise fx="code:@wipe_to_photo;clip:stock_startup_sketch_product_hands_01;pic:nike_vs_adidas_nike_air_max_97_boxes;go:formula;ty:.74;band:1;lab:VISIBLE AIR · SINCE 1987;sub:pictured: a later model, the Air Max 97;out:right"
The formula kept working. In nineteen eighty-seven, Nike put a window in the sole: Air **Max**.

// CARD a4-02 · SEE: real runners mid-stride in black and white; on "three" a placard is planted into the scene: JUST DO IT · 1988 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@sign_on_footage;clip:nike_vs_adidas_marathon_bw_motion;sign:JUST DO IT;sub:1988 · Wieden+Kennedy;go:three;x:.5;y:.42;in:right"
A year later came three words, written by its ad agency: Just Do **It**.
The first ad starred a runner named Walt **Stack**.

// CARD a4-03 · SEE: hard cuts on the words: a concert crowd reaching up through stage light on "Run-DMC", a crowd raising its hands on "Garden" · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@word_cuts;items:stock_event_hands_beams_01@Run-DMC@.5@.5@1.15~nike_vs_adidas_adidas_superstar_white@Garden@.5@.5@1.1;out:right"
Adidas found its audience somewhere else. In nineteen eighty-six, Run-DMC released "My **Adidas**."
At Madison Square Garden, fans held up their Superstars, and Adidas signed the **band**.
Adidas calls it the birth of non-athletic promotion in sporting **goods**. (J)

// CARD a4-04 · SEE: two framed prints: Phil Knight (NIKE) and a white sneaker (AI prop, REEBOK · 1987 · ABOUT $1.4B); on "Reebok" the Nike card sinks and greys while the Reebok card rises · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@rise_and_sink;down:nike_vs_adidas_phil_knight_football_sideline;up:nike_vs_adidas_el_sneaker_white;dlab:NIKE;ulab:REEBOK · 1987 (ILLUSTRATION);dat:scare;uat:Reebok;in:right"
Nike's real scare came from Reebok, which took the American lead in the late eighties on aerobics **shoes**.
But Nike took the lead back in nineteen eighty-**eight**.

// CARD a4-05 · SEE: dark floor; three solid blocks rise one after another, each taller: FY1986 $1.07B, FY1996 $6.47B, FY2003 $10.7B · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@blocks;vals:1.07,6.47,10.7;labs:FY1986,FY1996,FY2003;txts:$1.07B,$6.47B,$10.7B;go:yearly;gap:.7;note:Nike revenue · fiscal years to May · as reported;base:.8;notey:.085;in:right"
Nike's yearly sales passed one billion dollars in the mid-eighties.
They reached six and a half billion by nineteen ninety-six, and ten billion by two thousand **three**.

// CARD a4-06 · SEE: two real prints side by side, a Swoosh shoe and Michael Jordan; on "brand" the Swoosh print slides away and BRAND JORDAN · 1997 · THE JUMPMAN takes the light · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@shelf_switch;a:nike_vs_adidas_nike_football_shoes_pair;b:nike_vs_adidas_michael_jordan_white_house_2016;alab:THE SWOOSH;blab:BRAND JORDAN · 1997 · PHOTO 2016;go:brand;out:right"
In nineteen ninety-seven, Jordan got his own brand, with the Jumpman instead of the **Swoosh**.

// CARD a4-07 · SEE: the real Adidas outlet at Herzogenaurach; on "bought" a paper price tag swings in on a string: REEBOK · $3.8B · 2006 · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@valuation_tag;bg:nike_vs_adidas_adidas_factory_outlet_herzogenaurach;vals:$3.8B@bought;x:.66;lab:REEBOK · 2006;sub:to fight Nike in America;out:right"
So in two thousand six, Adidas bought Reebok for about three point eight billion dollars, to fight Nike in **America**.
It even dressed the NBA, until Nike took that contract over in twenty **seventeen**.

// CARD a4-08 · SEE: a real clothing store interior, dim; a wooden ruler 2013 to 2015 slides in; on "left" a red line runs across it: KANYE WEST · FROM NIKE TO ADIDAS · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@year_ruler;bg:nike_vs_adidas_sneakers_neon_wall;y0:2013;y1:2015;go:twenty;span:1.2;lab:KANYE WEST · FROM NIKE TO ADIDAS;out:right"
In twenty thirteen, Kanye West left Nike for Adidas. The first Yeezy Boost arrived in twenty **fifteen**.

// CARD a4-09 · SEE: NEW: a hundred grey shoes on a dim shelf grid; on "early" 11 light blue; on "still" 37 light red and 10 white (Jordan Brand); the shares set under the grid · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@share_shoes;bg:nike_vs_adidas_sneakers_thrift_shopping;groups:ADIDAS^11.3^nva_cut_sneaker_blue^#7fb2ff^early~NIKE^37^nva_cut_sneaker_red^#ff6b5e^climbed~JORDAN BRAND^9.5^nva_cut_sneaker_white^#f2f4f8^climbed;lab:US SNEAKER MARKET · JAN–AUG 2017;src:NPD Group data, as reported Sept 2017;in:right"
In early twenty seventeen, Adidas grew faster than Nike in North America, on Boost, Superstar and Stan **Smith**.
By twenty seventeen, Adidas had climbed to about eleven percent of the American sneaker **market**.
Nike still had thirty-seven **percent**.

// CARD a4-10 · SEE: MOTIF: the red shoe stands on its plank; on "Nike" the blue one runs up toward its own but stops short · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@lane_race;bg:nike_vs_adidas_art_running_track;a:nva_cut_sneaker_red;b:nva_cut_sneaker_blue;alab:NIKE;blab:ADIDAS;ax0:0.9;ax:0.9;bx0:0.4;bx:0.72;go:Nike;lab:NIKE · ON EVERY SHELF;in:right"
Nike didn't need to chase anyone. It was on every **shelf**.
{wry} So what could possibly go **wrong**? (J)
